  "narrative": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">This dataset shows an early but clear reuse signal: it has been cited once in the scholarly record and has two documented downstream reuse events. It is an openly available Figshare dataset assembling the genome, gene annotations, predicted transcripts, and predicted peptides for <i>Chromosphaera perkinsii</i>.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The record for <i>Genome - Chromosphaera perkinsii</i> centers on a genomic resource published on Figshare in 2017 by Multicellgenome Lab. The dataset provides genome assembly and annotation files for <i>Chromosphaera perkinsii</i>, supporting work in biology, genomics, computational biology, genetics, and gene-focused analysis. Its open-access status and repository placement make the data directly reusable for follow-on studies.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Evidence of impact is still modest, but it is meaningful. OpenAlex records one citation, and the reuse graph shows two downstream reuse signals, including one scholarly article mention and one biomedical article mention. The citing literature points to continued interest in protist diversity and phylogeny, indicating that the dataset is being drawn into broader comparative and evolutionary work rather than remaining a standalone repository item.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The citation landscape is narrow but specific: the documented citing work is an eLife article published in 2025 from a U.S.-based research context. That pattern suggests the dataset is being used as a foundational genomic reference in current research on the closest living relatives of metazoans. Because the record is small, it is best read as an emerging sign of uptake rather than established broad adoption.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Stewardship is strong. Figshare provides persistent access, the dataset is openly available through repository infrastructure recognized in OpenAIRE, and the resource is indexed with a DOI-based preservation workflow. No public discourse or altmetric-style discussion is captured here, so the strongest evidence remains the scholarly reuse already documented in citations and data-citation tracking.</p>",
